## Context

Author ruling, 2026-11-10 morning, on the weekly ticket audit's
recommendation: trim `scripts/gate_tree.py` ("2 yes trim"). The session that
received the ruling ("orphan session audit") had a coder look before writing,
then held the work because ticket 1500 edited the same script and the same
passage of `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md`. Ticket 1500 landed (PR #2394); that
session had ended by then and nothing was filed. This ticket records what its
coder found, as relayed to the raid on 1500, with the facts rechecked on
main after PR #2394.

Facts on main:
- `scripts/gate_tree.py` is 125 lines; `tests/test_gate_tree.py` is 194.
- No script calls it. `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md:117` does, and relies on
  exit 1 meaning ESCALATE and on the printed tree path.
- `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md:125` and
  `skills/raid/references/circuit-breakers.md:54` relaunch a stalled gate
  under the same session and rely on the stale same-round tree being replaced.

So the audit's premise holds only in part. The ticket 1490 fix is
instructions in skill text; a gate can still end with no verdict and leave
its tree, and the plain replace is still needed. What can go without losing
a rail:
- the verdict-state check (`ruled()`, `--gate-state`, `--session`, `--round`:
  refuse when the round already carries a verdict), about 20 lines;
- the name check (a tree whose name does not start with `gate-` is a usage
  error).
What stays: the lock refusal, the not-a-worktree refusal, the
unreachable-commit refusal, the residue refusal through the exit preflight
(which since PR #2394 also covers a nested registered worktree), and the
replace itself. About 25 lines of script and the tests that covered the two
checks go; `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md:117-130` is reworded to match.

Open for the planner: whether a 25-line trim is worth a full-tier review and
gate of gate machinery, or the ruling is better served by leaving the script
and closing this ticket as settled. The author ruled "trim" on the audit's
fuller premise; return it to the author if the reduced scope changes the call.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `gate_tree.py add` no longer takes `--gate-state`, `--session` or `--round` and no longer checks the tree's name; its callers' text in `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md` and `skills/raid` is brought in line
- [ ] A stale same-round gate tree is still replaced, and the lock, not-a-worktree, unreachable-commit and residue refusals still answer exit 1 with nothing removed, by the existing tests
- [ ] The tests that covered only the two removed checks are deleted; no test asserts wording in the script's or the skill's source
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine (raid 61f50c, 2026-11-10)

Explicit `/raid 1515` by the author with this ticket's open question in view:
read as confirming the "trim" ruling on the reduced scope.

Blind-spot pass (one read-only seat): the verdict-state check is
near-redundant: `review_notes.py gate` derives `round = len(verdicts)+1`
(scripts/review_notes.py ~1005-1028), so a ruled round maps to a new tree
path, standalone and REROLL round 2 alike; only a stale cross-host read with
the same session UUID could reuse one, not a normal flow. Material omission:
the name check is today the only tie between `--tree` and a gate tree; without
it a typo'd path to a clean, unlocked session or PR worktree on a pushed head
passes every kept refusal and is removed. Implementation choice (exit
criteria unchanged): replace the name check with an identity check on the
registered record: a stale tree is replaced only when it is detached
(`detached` in the porcelain record); a branch-attached worktree is refused,
exit 1, nothing removed. Gate trees are always `worktree add --detach`.

## Plan

Actions:
1. `scripts/gate_tree.py`: drop `ruled()`, the `json` import, `--session`,
   `--round`, `--gate-state` and the `gate-` name check; in `add()`, after the
   lock refusal, refuse a registered record without `detached`; update the
   docstring (refusal list, exit 2 line).
2. `tests/test_gate_tree.py`: helper `add()` stops passing the three flags;
   delete `test_stale_tree_with_a_verdict_of_another_session_or_round_is_replaced`,
   `test_recorded_verdict_for_the_session_and_round_refuses`,
   `test_a_tree_not_named_gate_is_refused`,
   `test_unreadable_gate_state_refuses_without_a_traceback`; module docstring
   drops the verdict clause.
3. First test (red before the code change): a registered worktree on a branch
   (`git worktree add -b x <path>`, clean, reachable HEAD) passed as `--tree`
   is refused exit 1 and still exists afterwards. Would catch a wrong
   implementation: today it passes only through the name check (exit 2), so
   name the tree `gate-...` in the test to make it red on main.
4. `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md` ~107-130: the call drops `--session`,
   `--round`, `--gate-state` and the `<gate-state>` comment (keep the
   `<round>` comment: it still names the path); the refusal sentence drops
   "when that session and round already carry a verdict" and adds the
   branch-attached refusal. `skills/raid/references/circuit-breakers.md` ~54
   likewise.
5. Keep `tests/test_verify_fork_contracts.py` pins intact (path line and
   `add --repo "$primary_root" --tree "$gate_tree"` survive). Add no wording test.

Dependencies: none. Feasibility: paths and line numbers checked on
origin/main be109f4a0 (PASS).
